---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../site.config';

const email = site.contact.email;
const updated = '19 November 2026';
const storage = [
  {
    name: 'bdb-globe-cookies',
    type: 'Essential, local storage in your browser',
    purpose: "Remembers your cookie choice so the banner doesn't ask again on every page. Never sent anywhere.",
    kept: '6 months, then you are asked again',
  },
  {
    name: '_ga',
    type: 'Analytics cookie (Google), only after Accept',
    purpose: 'Tells visits from different browsers apart with a random ID.',
    kept: '2 years',
  },
  {
    name: '_ga_<ID>',
    type: 'Analytics cookie (Google), only after Accept',
    purpose: 'Keeps track of your current visit (session).',
    kept: '2 years',
  },
];
---

<Base title="Privacy & cookies" description="How this site uses cookies and Google Analytics, and your rights under the GDPR." bodyClass="is-gallery-page">
  <main id="main" class="page legal">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="tag">Privacy</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">Privacy &amp; cookies</h1>
      <p class="legal-updated">Last updated {updated}</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose legal-prose">
      <p>
        This is a personal photography portfolio. It has no accounts, forms, shop or advertising. The only data it
        collects is visitor statistics through Google Analytics, and analytics cookies are only used if you accept them.
      </p>

      <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
      <p>
        I, Bryce de Bruyn (Barcelona, Spain), am responsible for this website and for the personal data described here
        (the data controller). Contact: <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>I use Google Analytics 4 to count visits and see which places and photos people look at. Nothing is used for advertising or to build a profile of you.</li>
        <li>Until you choose, and if you press <strong>Only essential</strong>, Google Analytics runs <strong>without cookies</strong>: it only receives anonymous visit counts that cannot recognise you or link your visits together.</li>
        <li>If you press <strong>Accept</strong>, Google Analytics may also set its cookies, so repeat visits can be counted.</li>
        <li>You can change your choice at any time with “Cookie settings” (in the menu, at the bottom of each gallery and below).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Google Analytics</h2>
      <p>Every visit loads Google Analytics 4 through the Google tag, using Google Consent Mode.</p>
      <p>
        <strong>Without consent (before you choose, or “Only essential”):</strong> no cookies are set or read. Google
        receives anonymous signals (“cookieless pings”) that a page was viewed or something was clicked, with the page,
        time, approximate country and city, and your device and browser type, but no ID that recognises you or connects
        one visit to another. Google uses them only to estimate visitor totals.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>With consent (“Accept”):</strong> Google Analytics also sets the cookies listed below. It then collects
        usage data such as the locations and galleries you open, how far you scroll, how long you stay, the website that
        sent you here, your approximate location (country and city, derived from your IP address), and your device,
        browser, operating system and screen size. Its cookies give your browser a random ID so repeat visits can be
        counted.
      </p>
      <p>
        Google Analytics 4 does not log or store IP addresses. Nothing is used for advertising: this site always tells
        Google that ad storage, ad user data and ad personalisation are refused.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Legal basis:</strong> for analytics cookies, your consent (GDPR art. 6(1)(a) and art. 22.2 of the
          Spanish LSSI). You can withdraw it at any time, as easily as you gave it; this does not affect anything processed
          before. For the anonymous counts without cookies, my legitimate interest in knowing how many people visit my
          work (GDPR art. 6(1)(f)). You can object to it by emailing me, or block it with a browser content blocker.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Who processes it:</strong> Google Ireland Limited (Gordon House, Barrow Street, Dublin 4, Ireland), as
          my processor. Data may be transferred to Google LLC in the United States, which is certified under the EU–US
          Data Privacy Framework; Google's standard contractual clauses also apply.
        </li>
        <li><strong>How long:</strong> Google Analytics keeps visit-level data for 14 months; after that only totals remain.</li>
        <li>
          More: <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites" target="_blank" rel="noopener">how Google uses data from sites that use its services</a>{' '}
          and <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google's privacy policy</a>.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Cookies and browser storage</h2>
      <div class="legal-table-wrap">
        <table class="legal-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Name</th><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">Purpose</th><th scope="col">Kept for</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              storage.map((c) => (
                <tr>
                  <td class="mono" data-label="Name">{c.name}</td>
                  <td data-label="Type">{c.type}</td>
                  <td data-label="Purpose">{c.purpose}</td>
                  <td data-label="Kept for">{c.kept}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        When you choose “Only essential” after having accepted, the site deletes the <span class="mono">_ga</span>{' '}
        cookies it can reach. You can also block or delete cookies in your browser settings at any time.
      </p>

      <h2>Your choices</h2>
      <p>
        You can change your analytics choice at any time:{' '}
        <button class="text-btn" type="button" data-consent-open>open cookie settings</button>.
      </p>

      <h2>Hosting</h2>
      <p>
        The site is hosted on GitHub Pages, run by GitHub, Inc. (United States). Like any web server, GitHub receives
        technical information when you load a page, such as your IP address, browser and the page requested, and may
        keep it in server logs for security and to run the service. I don't have access to these logs. This is needed to
        deliver the site (legal basis: legitimate interest, GDPR art. 6(1)(f)). See{' '}
        <a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub's privacy statement</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>No other third parties</h2>
      <p>
        Apart from Google Analytics, everything the site needs, including fonts, photos and the live ISS orbit data, is
        served from this site itself, so your browser doesn't contact other companies unless you follow an external link
        (for example to YouTube or LinkedIn), where that site's own privacy policy applies.
      </p>

      <h2>Emails you send me</h2>
      <p>
        If you email me, I receive your email address and message in my email account (Gmail, provided by Google) and
        use them only to reply to you. I keep them only as long as needed for our conversation.
      </p>

      <h2>Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        Under the GDPR you have the right to access, correct or delete your personal data, to restrict or object to its
        processing, to data portability, and to withdraw consent at any time. To use these rights, email{' '}
        <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>. Because Google Analytics only uses a random ID, I usually cannot link
        the data to you personally, so I may need details such as the date and time of your visit to find it.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you think your data has been handled improperly, you can complain to the Spanish data protection authority,
        the <a href="https://www.aepd.es" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Agencia Española de Protección de Datos (AEPD)</a>,
        or to the authority in your own country.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>If this site starts collecting anything new, this page will be updated first and the date at the top will change.</p>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>
